# Project Architecture: Feature-Driven Architecture (FDA)

This project uses a strict Feature-Driven Architecture. Follow these rules for EVERY file you create or modify.

## Layer Hierarchy (top → bottom, imports flow downward only)

```
app/ → widgets/ → features/ → entities/ → shared/
```

**Hard rules:**
- `app/` contains ONLY routing files (page.tsx, layout.tsx, route.ts). Zero business logic.
- `features/` contain ALL business logic. One folder per domain (auth, dashboard, billing, etc.)
- `shared/` contains ZERO business logic — only pure utils, UI primitives, lib configs
- A feature NEVER imports from another feature directly
- `shared/` NEVER imports from `features/`

## When creating a new feature

Always scaffold the full structure:

```
src/features/[name]/
├── components/   ← UI only used inside this feature
├── hooks/        ← custom hooks (combine store + queries)
├── store/        ← Zustand slice (client/UI state only)
├── api/          ← React Query hooks (server state)
├── types/        ← feature-scoped TypeScript types
├── utils/        ← pure functions, no side effects
└── index.ts      ← barrel file, public API only
```

## index.ts barrel rule

Every feature MUST have an `index.ts`. Only export what other layers need.
Never export internal implementation details (raw store, internal hooks, api internals).

```ts
// GOOD
export { LoginForm } from './components/LoginForm'
export { useSession } from './hooks/useSession'
export type { AuthUser } from './types/auth.types'

// BAD — don't export internals
export { useAuthStore } from './store/authStore'
export { authApi } from './api/authQueries'
```

## Page files must be thin

```tsx
// GOOD — page just composes features
import { DashboardView } from '@/features/dashboard'
export default function Page() { return <DashboardView /> }

// BAD — page contains logic
export default function Page() {
  const [data, setData] = useState([])
  useEffect(() => { fetch(...).then(setData) }, [])
  return <div>{data.map(...)}</div>
}
```

## Path aliases

Always use `@/` aliases, never relative `../../`:
- `@/features/auth`
- `@/shared/ui`
- `@/entities/user`
- `@/widgets/Navbar`
